[x] Cascade complete keeps existing closures =complete-keeps-closed $Found by review 20261031-223325/6 of =cancel-keeps-completed, which was cancellation-only. Completing a parent still rewrites an already-cancelled child as completed with today's timestamp, the same history loss. Reopen also resets every subtree member to not-started, including members that kept their completed state through a cancel. NEEDS DECISION: should complete and reopen preserve closed descendants as cancel now does? process.md allows deliberately requested cascades, so this may touch what the specification says. Also from review 20261031-223325/8: transact complete-action on a terminal target still in the active file succeeds and restamps the subtree, while ordinary complete rejects it and transact cancel-action now does too. DECIDED by Darrion 2026-11-01: 'if i complete in a casacding fashion any existing closures or cancells stay so if a chid has a closed state dont overwrite it.' Scope: (1) close_subtree in domain/mod.rs keeps state and completed_at of closed descendants for complete as it now does for cancel, tests first, through ordinary complete and transact complete-action. (2) transact complete-action on a target already terminal in the active file returns AlreadyClosed, as ordinary complete and transact cancel-action do, since restamping it overwrites a closure. (3) Fix agent items left open from review 20261031-223325/8: share the terminal-target guard between adapters (8#2) and move transaction_cancel_terminal.rs into transaction.rs's tests (8#3). Check process.md and record the rule in docs/DECISIONS.md; process.md's cascade text does not appear to say otherwise, so this should not change the specification, and if it does, stop and ask. Reopen is NOT in scope; it is a separate decision. Implemented 2026-11-01. Tests-first commit clearhead-core 977aee5 demonstrated history loss through ordinary complete and transact complete-action, terminal-target acceptance, and the generated closure invariant. Implementation a6622c2 preserves closed states and timestamps (including absent timestamps), shares the terminal-target guard across ordinary preparation/CLI and transaction close, and relocates transaction_cancel_terminal.rs cases into transaction.rs unit tests. Rule recorded in clearhead-core/docs/DECISIONS.md Decision 2 after checking process.md closure and child-action text; no specification or reopen change. Full sh scripts/gate.sh passes; doctor remains at 0 violations and the same 13 pre-existing warnings. No new dependencies.$ !3 ^2026-10-31T22:48 %2026-11-01T18:50 #01a19426-2ca5-7272-b7ee-3df678d9b09b
[x] Reopen keeps closures inside a reopened subtree? $⏸ Decision for Darrion, from =complete-keeps-closed. Reopening a parent drives every action in its subtree to not-started and clears completed_at (domain/mod.rs reopen_subtree), so a child that was closed on its own before the parent closed comes back open and loses its date. Darrion decided on 2026-11-01 that cascading complete and cancel keep existing closures, and did not address reopen. Question: should reopening a parent also leave children that were already closed alone? NEEDS DECISION. Recommendation held until Darrion answers. ANSWERED by Darrion 2026-11-01; design goes to =close-individually.$ !3 +human ^2026-11-01T18:39 %2026-11-01T19:10 #01a19868-bfb5-7de0-821a-a2839d813b4c
[x] Does plain complete count as an explicit batch command? =complete-explicit-batch $⏸ Decision for Darrion, from review 20261101-184122/4 of =complete-keeps-closed. specifications/process.md, On Child Actions: 'An explicit command may offer a reviewed batch transition, but merely completing or cancelling one Action must not rewrite its relatives.' Yet plain clearhead complete action <parent> closes the parent's open descendants with no flag and no review step, and Core Decision 2 calls this an explicit reviewed batch transition. The tension predates =complete-keeps-closed, which only reduces what the cascade rewrites. Your 2026-11-01 answer spoke of completing 'in a cascading fashion', which does not settle whether the plain verb should cascade by default. Question: is the plain verb the explicit batch command the specification allows, or must a cascade be asked for separately? One answer changes the CLI, the other may change the specification's wording. NEEDS DECISION. Recommendation held until Darrion answers. ANSWERED by Darrion 2026-11-01; design goes to =close-individually.$ !3 +human ^2026-11-01T19:08 %2026-11-01T19:10 #01a19883-512f-7a52-8f55-6f53a24eb562
[x] Darrion\: should archive action on a closed child detach it, rather than refuse? =archive-child $⏸ Decision for Darrion, from =close-individually, not blocking it. Darrion stated the root-only rule for unarchive; for archive it is an inference. Being built as written: refuse and name the root, so a closed child stays in the active file until its root tree closes. The other way: detach it and archive it as its own root, which keeps the active file tidier, loses the parent link, and it can only return as a new root. Refusing can be relaxed later without breaking anyone. NEEDS DECISION.$ !3 +human ^2026-11-02T02:38 %2026-11-02T02:56 #01a19a1f-c086-7ea1-85a8-dd20346e32ac
